# Base change of the q-expansion function field

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange_qExpFunctionFieldC_eq`
(`5920f53d-ca89-5169-bd10-9d9a0ae8a631`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `9012c10f-067f-57da-a3c0-eab06f24dd7c`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $L$ be a field equipped with a $\mathbf{Q}$-algebra structure and let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of
$\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbf{Z})$. For a field $K$ write $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K$ for the map sending an
integral power series to its image in $K((q))$ (coefficients pushed along $\mathbf{Z} \to K$, then
viewed as a Laurent series), and let [`ModularCurve.intFormRatiosC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L83)
$K\,\Gamma \subseteq K((q))$ be the set of quotients $\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_f /
\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g$ where $k$ is an integer, $f$ and $g$ are modular forms of weight $k$
for the image of $\Gamma$ in $\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbf{R})$, $p_f, p_g$ are power series over
$\mathbf{Z}$ satisfying `IsIntegralQExp` for $f$ and for $g$ respectively, and
$\mathrm{intSeriesC}\,K\,p_g \neq 0$; and let
[`ModularCurve.qExpFunctionFieldC`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L101) $K\,\Gamma$ be the intermediate
field of $K((q))/K$ generated by this set. Let
[`ModularCurve.coeffEmb`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) $L$ be the ring homomorphism
$\mathbf{Q}((q)) \to L((q))$ applying $\mathbf{Q} \to L$ to each coefficient. The assertion is that
the intermediate field of $L((q))/L$ generated by the `coeffEmb` $L$-image of `qExpFunctionFieldC`
$\mathbf{Q}\,\Gamma$, that is
[`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) $L$ applied to it,
coincides with `qExpFunctionFieldC` $L\,\Gamma$.

This identifies the compositum $L \cdot F(\Gamma)$ inside $L((q))$ with the field $F_L(\Gamma)$
built directly over $L$ from the same integral $q$-expansions, so that results phrased with either
construction of the $q$-expansion function field of a modular curve may be used interchangeably. It
is invoked by the statements about function fields of $X_1(M)$ and $X_H(M)$ over a general
coefficient field, where the two descriptions must be matched.

